<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Tsushima-Fuchu Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1871 CE · capital Izuhara (Fuchu)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The island domain of the So family, Japan's sole intermediary with Joseon Korea through the Edo period.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The So family had ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsushima-island">Tsushima</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, since the 13th century and depended on trade with Korea. After the Japanese invasions of Korea in the 1590s, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/so-yoshitoshi">So Yoshitoshi</a> worked to restore relations, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> confirmed his domain in 1600.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>To secure peace the So forged diplomatic letters between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> and the Korean king, which led to the Korean embassy of 1607; the forgery was exposed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanagawa-affair">Yanagawa affair</a> of 1635 but the So kept their role. They staffed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> House at Busan, monopolised trade in Korean ginseng, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, and escorted the Korean embassies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Edo</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1861 a Russian warship occupied part of the island until British pressure forced it out. After abolition in 1871 the domain became Izuhara Prefecture and was later joined to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> Prefecture, and Korean relations passed to the Meiji foreign ministry.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tsushima-fuchu-domain.jpg" alt="Tsushima-Fuchu Domain" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: LERK · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tsushima_island_en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Izuhara (Fuchu)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>So Yoshitoshi, So Yoshinari</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Shinto</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tsushima Domain, Fuchu Domain</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=tsushima-fuchu-domain" aria-label="Tsushima-Fuchu Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7971 938 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tsushima-Fuchu Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8291 1194L8530 1145M8291 1194L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8291 1194h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8291 1194h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Izuhara. <a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=tsushima-fuchu-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
